  "method": {
    "workload": "Each request rereads one committed 9,000,000-row upload. PostgreSQL restarted once after seed and checkpoint; OS cache not cleared; fresh API container per group; one host client process per group.",
    "sdk": "Public SDK streams and validates every row and checks row numbers against 1..9000000; benchmark measures SHA-256 and byte count in the HTTP transport, without retaining rows or decoding twice.",
    "raw_control": "Current stand, four concurrent requests, historical byte-counting script with the same SHA-256 verification and no JSON decoding.",
    "timing": "HTTP readiness and receipt outside the timer; barrier coordinates concurrent starts; group timing ends after the last export completes. Report distinguishes first body bytes from the first decoded row. Single-stream figures use the median of two runs.",
    "cpu": "Seconds from the change in each Docker cgroup usage_usec or Python process_time for the client, divided by group wall time for average cores. Docker counters bracket the client invocation and include measurement overhead and incidental database activity.",
    "memory": "API PID 1 VmHWM, client process ru_maxrss, and samples about every 0.5 s of PostgreSQL container memory.current (includes page cache charged to the container cgroup) and sum of RssAnon for application-role PostgreSQL backends (avoids summing shared RSS). Active backend samples are process titles other than idle, including idle in transaction between cursor fetches.",
    "client_memory_comparability": "The raw benchmark also imports asyncpg for seeding; compare process peaks as measured and use the SDK-only series to assess memory scaling.",
    "postgres_io": "pg_stat_database deltas include diagnostics and background activity. Buffer misses may be served from the OS page cache."
  },
